WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:01.660
Hello, I'm Gavin.

00:00:01.660 --> 00:00:04.410
Welcome to this solo-hosted
Slow Mo Guys video.

00:00:04.410 --> 00:00:07.250
This video is sponsored by LG.

00:00:07.250 --> 00:00:09.930
And to help us make it,
they've kindly provided

00:00:09.930 --> 00:00:12.190
this colossal television,

00:00:12.190 --> 00:00:14.550
which is currently
displaying the live feed

00:00:14.550 --> 00:00:17.460
of a Phantom camera, sat right over there.

00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:19.750
So why don't we take a look at the setup.

00:00:19.750 --> 00:00:21.720
There is a set of stills photos online,

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:25.040
by a German photographer
called Markus Reugels,

00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:26.870
I think is how you pronounce his name.

00:00:26.870 --> 00:00:29.090
He's done a bunch of droplet photography.

00:00:29.090 --> 00:00:31.210
So as the droplet falls into the water,

00:00:31.210 --> 00:00:34.670
and then rises back up and
separates from its column,

00:00:34.670 --> 00:00:37.970
he'll take a picture, and
it will refract an image,

00:00:37.970 --> 00:00:39.610
through the droplet behind.

00:00:39.610 --> 00:00:41.940
So he's done the earth and other planets.

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:43.700
And we're gonna basically replicate that

00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:45.150
but have a video element,

00:00:45.150 --> 00:00:46.700
so you can actually see it in motion

00:00:46.700 --> 00:00:49.130
and hopefully see the
separation of the droplet,

00:00:49.130 --> 00:00:51.690
and it'll just look like the planet Earth.

00:00:51.690 --> 00:00:54.450
As a little globe in a
tiny droplet of water.

00:00:54.450 --> 00:00:56.690
Now, in order to make this work,

00:00:56.690 --> 00:00:59.050
we need to flip the world upside down,

00:00:59.050 --> 00:01:00.530
and that is because the droplet of water

00:01:00.530 --> 00:01:02.490
will be flipping the image.

00:01:02.490 --> 00:01:04.640
So, all of the information down here

00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:06.850
will be seen in the top of the droplet,

00:01:06.850 --> 00:01:08.680
and all the information from up here,

00:01:08.680 --> 00:01:09.930
will be seen the bottom.

00:01:09.930 --> 00:01:11.610
Kind of like how your eyeballs work.

00:01:11.610 --> 00:01:14.810
Now I didn't actually have a
big map of the earth like this,

00:01:14.810 --> 00:01:18.060
so I printed one out, it's
like 15 pieces of paper,

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:19.940
cut them out and stuck into this board.

00:01:19.940 --> 00:01:21.030
It looks pretty terrible,

00:01:21.030 --> 00:01:22.320
but hopefully because it will be seen

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:24.230
through a droplet about here,

00:01:24.230 --> 00:01:27.340
you won't be able to notice
how shoddy my map is.

00:01:27.340 --> 00:01:30.060
We'll start at 12 and a half
thousand frames a second,

00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:32.810
and the V2640 which means
we need a lot of light,

00:01:32.810 --> 00:01:34.160
let me stop you down there.

00:01:35.410 --> 00:01:39.223
And this could take many, many attempts.

00:01:45.610 --> 00:01:47.140
Because this is such a fiddly shot,

00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:49.600
and this camera's very
loud to stand next to,

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:52.030
I'm gonna play it back on the 8K OLED.

00:01:52.030 --> 00:01:52.863
While we watch these back,

00:01:52.863 --> 00:01:54.570
I'm gonna turn off the on-screen display

00:01:54.570 --> 00:01:57.063
so, we don't have anything fixed.

00:02:00.760 --> 00:02:03.010
So this time I focused
on the droplet itself.

00:02:04.620 --> 00:02:06.740
Yeah, and it does seem like the map

00:02:07.690 --> 00:02:10.240
is too far away from the droplet,

00:02:10.240 --> 00:02:11.430
to be in focus at the same time.

00:02:11.430 --> 00:02:13.070
So, I'm gonna have to figure out a way

00:02:13.070 --> 00:02:14.393
to add some depth here.

00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:16.550
I do love how

00:02:17.750 --> 00:02:18.760
the 12,000 frames,

00:02:18.760 --> 00:02:21.543
the separation here should
be really, really nice.

00:02:22.430 --> 00:02:23.630
What do you think Smith?

00:02:24.770 --> 00:02:26.520
Oh, yeah, do you like that?

00:02:26.520 --> 00:02:27.520
It's cool, ain't it?

00:02:28.600 --> 00:02:30.287
What do you think of the droplet?

00:02:30.287 --> 00:02:32.369
What do you think of the droplet?

00:02:32.369 --> 00:02:33.755
It's good, ain't it?

00:02:33.755 --> 00:02:34.588
Yeah!

00:02:41.773 --> 00:02:43.900
So this is the opposite,
the map is in focus,

00:02:43.900 --> 00:02:46.773
but the actual definition of
the droplet is very blurry.

00:02:56.280 --> 00:02:58.720
That one went a little bit
too high, just out of frame.

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:00.733
So I'm just gonna tilt up, try again.

00:03:04.210 --> 00:03:05.750
So stopped the lens down here,

00:03:05.750 --> 00:03:07.620
that's why it's a lot darker.

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:09.840
But it has brought this
map slightly more in focus,

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:11.770
I think we have to stop
the lens down even further,

00:03:11.770 --> 00:03:15.620
to get that sharp-looking,
which will make it even darker.

00:03:15.620 --> 00:03:18.240
But, this is currently
at 12,000 frames a second

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:21.370
and I think it might
be slower than we need.

00:03:21.370 --> 00:03:24.723
God I love the way it
stacks them like that.

00:03:25.600 --> 00:03:26.710
So, we could probably get away

00:03:26.710 --> 00:03:30.230
with dropping it to 6000 frames a second,

00:03:30.230 --> 00:03:32.080
and stopping the lens down even more.

00:03:33.050 --> 00:03:34.483
Reset the old syringe here.

00:03:39.830 --> 00:03:41.863
This is 6000 frames a second.

00:03:42.910 --> 00:03:45.480
so we've gained an entire stop of light,

00:03:45.480 --> 00:03:47.060
and I've been able to add
a little bit more depth,

00:03:47.060 --> 00:03:49.370
it's still dark, so I'm
gonna add another light.

00:03:49.370 --> 00:03:50.893
But this is just testing it.

00:03:51.980 --> 00:03:54.090
Yeah, so now the actual droplet itself

00:03:54.090 --> 00:03:56.183
is showing sharpness as well as the map.

00:03:58.400 --> 00:04:00.550
Getting there, wanna get
just a bit more light now,

00:04:00.550 --> 00:04:04.890
so I'm gonna turn on
this very bright torch,

00:04:04.890 --> 00:04:07.653
which may result in an
extremely bright Africa.

00:04:10.980 --> 00:04:11.870
To my eye at the time,

00:04:11.870 --> 00:04:14.503
this one made the map look
the sharpest I've seen.

00:04:17.480 --> 00:04:19.100
Definitely at the cost of the sharpness

00:04:19.100 --> 00:04:20.200
of the droplet though.

00:04:21.260 --> 00:04:25.270
Wow, you can actually see how
bad my map is in this one,

00:04:25.270 --> 00:04:26.370
you can see that line.

00:04:27.890 --> 00:04:29.220
At the cost of even more speed,

00:04:29.220 --> 00:04:31.990
I think I've got the map and the droplet

00:04:31.990 --> 00:04:34.340
as sharp as I want them at the same time.

00:04:34.340 --> 00:04:35.900
We're only 3000 frames a second though,

00:04:35.900 --> 00:04:38.313
so it is a little bit briefer.

00:04:39.330 --> 00:04:41.060
Still long enough to see it.

00:04:41.060 --> 00:04:41.893
Oh,

00:04:43.558 --> 00:04:44.523
I'm liking this.

00:04:54.459 --> 00:04:56.940
I really enjoy videos like
this, just experimental ones

00:04:56.940 --> 00:05:00.010
where you can just change
one variable each time.

00:05:00.010 --> 00:05:02.870
Currently, the only light is on the map,

00:05:02.870 --> 00:05:05.470
and I'm wondering whether to get this

00:05:05.470 --> 00:05:08.150
to appear a little bit
more spherical in 3D,

00:05:08.150 --> 00:05:10.220
I should actually shine some light

00:05:10.220 --> 00:05:13.110
to give it all highlights
on the edges of the droplet.

00:05:13.110 --> 00:05:16.280
So maybe hitting the droplet
with some light at some point,

00:05:16.280 --> 00:05:17.650
might be worth doing.

00:05:17.650 --> 00:05:18.770
So now I've taken the light

00:05:18.770 --> 00:05:20.320
that was blasting the top of Africa,

00:05:20.320 --> 00:05:23.150
and I've shined it
directly on the droplet.

00:05:23.150 --> 00:05:25.890
So we have these glistening parts,

00:05:25.890 --> 00:05:28.710
it looks more like a marble, wow.

00:05:28.710 --> 00:05:29.880
Not sure if that's what we want,

00:05:29.880 --> 00:05:31.830
but that's definitely a different look.

00:05:33.850 --> 00:05:36.680
Not a huge fan of all
these glasses putting in.

00:05:36.680 --> 00:05:37.513
I think for the next shot,

00:05:37.513 --> 00:05:39.530
I'm gonna get off this macro lens,

00:05:39.530 --> 00:05:41.010
and just pull back slightly,

00:05:41.010 --> 00:05:43.650
maybe get a shot of the
glass, a bit of a wider shot

00:05:43.650 --> 00:05:46.050
and the map a little bit more in focus.

00:05:46.050 --> 00:05:47.190
So this will now allow me just to be

00:05:47.190 --> 00:05:48.023
a little bit further back,

00:05:48.023 --> 00:05:50.320
see the whole fall of the
droplet, see the crown,

00:05:50.320 --> 00:05:53.380
see the column the
separation of the droplet,

00:05:53.380 --> 00:05:56.130
and also the entire glass
and the map at the same time.

00:06:00.900 --> 00:06:03.000
It's a lot easier when it's not macro.

00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:04.723
Back at 12,000 frames.

00:06:32.270 --> 00:06:34.440
Yeah, I feel like 12,000 is actually

00:06:34.440 --> 00:06:36.620
pretty perfect for this.

00:06:36.620 --> 00:06:39.380
It's incredibly leisurely,
like it's not too slobby,

00:06:39.380 --> 00:06:41.530
really do see everything's
happening there.

00:06:42.950 --> 00:06:44.440
And the shot has a lot more context,

00:06:44.440 --> 00:06:46.773
than the super duper macro stuff.

00:06:48.650 --> 00:06:51.360
So far there's still, in every shot,

00:06:51.360 --> 00:06:54.120
there's been sort of like a black border,

00:06:54.120 --> 00:06:54.953
on the whole thing,

00:06:54.953 --> 00:06:56.840
And I think that's mainly because

00:06:56.840 --> 00:06:58.790
my light is so close to the map,

00:06:58.790 --> 00:07:00.300
it's leaving very dark areas

00:07:00.300 --> 00:07:02.630
which I kind of need the
light to be that close,

00:07:02.630 --> 00:07:04.460
although it's not intense enough.

00:07:04.460 --> 00:07:06.840
Such the glaring error in the shot.

00:07:06.840 --> 00:07:08.600
The light that's lighting me over there,

00:07:08.600 --> 00:07:10.540
is not a high speed
light, is two low-power

00:07:10.540 --> 00:07:13.350
so it's flickering
because of the AC current.

00:07:13.350 --> 00:07:15.680
And you can see it in the reflection.

00:07:15.680 --> 00:07:18.633
That is as high speed 101 really,

00:07:19.658 --> 00:07:20.491
up

00:07:21.590 --> 00:07:24.010
Next up, I'm gonna take this lens off,

00:07:24.010 --> 00:07:25.690
and the mount off as well.

00:07:25.690 --> 00:07:29.330
So it accepts a PL lens,
one of these cinema lenses.

00:07:29.330 --> 00:07:31.670
This a 200 mil, so I'm gonna put this on,

00:07:31.670 --> 00:07:34.740
move the camera really far
back to its minimum focus.

00:07:34.740 --> 00:07:37.820
And what that should do,
is bring the background

00:07:37.820 --> 00:07:41.213
and the droplet, closer
together in the shot.

00:08:00.210 --> 00:08:02.773
Perfect.

00:08:10.945 --> 00:08:13.900
Never in my life had a monitor this big.

00:08:13.900 --> 00:08:15.950
Just gonna top up the water a little bit.

00:08:23.880 --> 00:08:26.373
I can see stuff floating in it.

00:08:59.550 --> 00:09:01.740
So now I'd love to tell you
a little bit about this TV.

00:09:01.740 --> 00:09:06.740
This is the world's first
LG signature 8K OLED TV.

00:09:07.990 --> 00:09:10.040
It's 88 inches diagonally,

00:09:10.040 --> 00:09:14.600
which is bigger than my wingspan.

00:09:14.600 --> 00:09:15.700
I don't think you'll ever get old

00:09:15.700 --> 00:09:18.120
that they use the screenshot
from one of our videos,

00:09:18.120 --> 00:09:21.253
as the YouTube sign in page on Android TV.

00:09:22.470 --> 00:09:24.970
I literally just press
YouTube and that popped up.

00:09:24.970 --> 00:09:28.400
Let's put the resolution
of 8K into perspective.

00:09:28.400 --> 00:09:32.420
If this was an HDTV, it
would be 1920 by 1080 pixels

00:09:32.420 --> 00:09:34.230
which is about two megapixels.

00:09:34.230 --> 00:09:39.090
And if it was you HD, it
would be 3840 by 2160,

00:09:39.090 --> 00:09:41.490
which is about eight megapixels.

00:09:41.490 --> 00:09:45.870
This TV is 7680 by 4320,

00:09:48.280 --> 00:09:50.653
which is about 33 megapixels.

00:09:51.740 --> 00:09:54.530
Every frame is 33 megapixels.

00:09:54.530 --> 00:09:57.160
Just for laughs, I've
connected a PC to it,

00:09:57.160 --> 00:10:00.880
and I've set the
resolution to 7680 by 4320

00:10:00.880 --> 00:10:04.040
and it currently has scaling of 300%,

00:10:04.040 --> 00:10:06.210
which makes everything look normal sized.

00:10:06.210 --> 00:10:07.570
I'm just gonna set to 100%,

00:10:07.570 --> 00:10:09.880
'cause then everything
will be pixel for pixel

00:10:09.880 --> 00:10:12.370
at 8K resolution which...

00:10:12.370 --> 00:10:14.520
Well, that should be
comical, to be honest.

00:10:20.240 --> 00:10:21.740
See how big the start menu is?

00:10:24.310 --> 00:10:25.223
So small!

00:10:26.640 --> 00:10:31.180
You could honestly sit here
like this, do some work.

00:10:31.180 --> 00:10:32.050
Now, you might be wondering,

00:10:32.050 --> 00:10:35.123
what can I watch in 8K
on a screen like this?

00:10:36.160 --> 00:10:37.150
Well, let me show you.

00:10:37.150 --> 00:10:39.770
We've actually been shooting
a lot of our videos in 8K,

00:10:39.770 --> 00:10:40.750
for quite some time.

00:10:40.750 --> 00:10:43.530
So, you can watch our videos if you want.

00:10:43.530 --> 00:10:46.423
This one's available in 4320p50.

00:10:48.760 --> 00:10:49.733
See how it looks.

00:10:52.220 --> 00:10:53.973
If my GPU doesn't explode.

00:10:55.840 --> 00:10:58.140
I can see the bald spot
on the top of my head.

00:11:00.170 --> 00:11:02.550
That's the downside of 8K I guess.

00:11:02.550 --> 00:11:04.670
In the last TV video I was showing

00:11:04.670 --> 00:11:07.360
how an LCD panel looked
different to an OLED panel,

00:11:07.360 --> 00:11:09.310
under a macro lens.

00:11:09.310 --> 00:11:12.730
This is what the 77
inch 4K OLED looked like

00:11:12.730 --> 00:11:16.140
under five times magnification
through the macro lens.

00:11:16.140 --> 00:11:18.870
And just to show the
increased pixel density,

00:11:18.870 --> 00:11:23.220
this is the 8K, 88 inch OLED
under five times magnification.

00:11:23.220 --> 00:11:24.490
And that just about does it.

00:11:24.490 --> 00:11:26.280
Hopefully you enjoyed that footage,

00:11:26.280 --> 00:11:29.470
big old thanks to LG for
sponsoring this video.

00:11:29.470 --> 00:11:31.780
And do make sure you
subscribe to The Slow Mo Guys.

00:11:31.780 --> 00:11:34.423
We have a second channel
too, I recommend it.

